        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Summary Agent"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "diagram": {
            "x": 7408.5,
            "y": 6094
          },
          "code": "SUMMARY_AGENT",
          "id": "1775735470234",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1775735470234_prompt",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "Role: You are a supplier compliance and regulatory intelligence agent for procurement and risk teams.\n\nInput : You are given few external resources summaries. These summaries along with their purpose given in the input {{$context.$nodes.EXTERNAL_SUMMARY_LLM.$output}} as resources.\n\nTask:\nAnalyze input and produce a single paragraph executive summary combining internal compliance risk with external regulatory impact.\n\nOutput Rules (Strict):\n1) paragraph must be 1ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Å“2 sentences\n2) No bullet points\n3) No headings\n4) No URLs\n5) Plain English only\n\nParagraph Logic:\n1) Start with ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œAlert ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Å“ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¯Â¿Â½.\n2) In one or two sentence, highlight the regulatory or standards change from the data provided in Input.\n3) Include:\n   3.1) What and when is the change\n   3.2) Effective timeframe\n   3.3) Impacted supplier types\n4) Show the source of the news along with the Purpose.\n5) End with one clear preventive action suppliers should take.\n\n\nTone & Quality Constraints:\n1) Executive-level clarity\n2) Actionable and risk-focused\n3) No assumptions beyond the inputs\n4) No repetition across paragraphs\n\nSuccess Criteria:\nA compliance leader should be able to:\n1) Understand risk concentration in under 10 seconds\n2) Identify what regulation is coming and why it matters\n\n\nOutput Example (for reference only):\nÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã…â€œAlert ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Å“ U.S. Food and Drug Administration QMSR Transition (Effective Feb 2026) ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Å“ FDA is aligning Quality System Regulation with ISO 13485. Device suppliers should complete a QMS gap assessment and update documentation to avoid future non-compliance.ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¯Â¿Â½"
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Data Classification Agent"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1766391860862"
          },
          "diagram": {
            "x": 6064,
            "y": 8146
          },
          "code": "DATA_CLASSIFICATION_AGENT",
          "id": "1776073859604",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1776072559774_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "You are a data aggregation agent. You will receive strings as input: {{$context.$variables.TierA}} = {{$context.$nodes.TIER_A_CLASSIFICATION_CODE.$output.result}}\n{{$context.$variables.TierB}} = {{$context.$nodes.TIER_B_CLASSIFICATION_CODE.$output.result}}\n{{$context.$variables.TierC}} = {{$context.$nodes.TIER_C_CLASSIFICATION_CODE.$output.result}}\n\nTask: Each string has 3 numeric values separated by a comma(\",\"). Split those 3 numeric values and consider them as\ncompliantCount, nonCompliantCount and noQualificationsCount respectively.\n\nReturn a JSON object including these numbers compliantCount, nonCompliantCount and noQualificationsCount for all the inputs,\nwhere the keys are the actual values of {{$context.$variables.TierA}}, {{$context.$variables.TierB}}, and {{$context.$variables.TierC}} instead of \"Group A\", \"Group B\", and \"Group C\".\n\nFor example, the output should be:\n{\n  \"{{$context.$variables.TierA}}\": {\n    \"compliantCount\": 107,\n    \"nonCompliantCount\": 53,\n    \"noQualificationsCount\": 1\n  },\n  \"{{$context.$variables.TierB}}\": {\n    \"compliantCount\": 5,\n    \"nonCompliantCount\": 203,\n    \"noQualificationsCount\": 21\n  },\n  \"{{$context.$variables.TierC}}\": {\n    \"compliantCount\": 3,\n    \"nonCompliantCount\": 157,\n    \"noQualificationsCount\": 15\n  }\n}\n\nDo not ask clarifying questions."
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "aiAppOutputSpecification": {
            "dataDisplay": {
              "layouts": [
                "ORA_LAYOUT_CHART"
              ],
              "enableActions": false,
              "enableCommunications": false
            }
          },
          "metadata": {
            "name": "SuppliersQualificationsSummaryLLM"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "diagram": {
            "x": 6035.5,
            "y": 8368
          },
          "code": "SUPPLIERSQUALIFICATIONSSUMMARYLLM",
          "convergenceTargetId": "1766397787173",
          "id": "1766391860862",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1766391860865_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "I will provide data in a JSON format for 3 groups. \nEach group contains 'compliantCount' and 'nonCompliantCount' integers.\nData Structure Reference: { \"GroupA\": {\"compliantCount\": X, \"nonCompliantCount\": Y, \"noQualificationsCount\": Z}, \"GroupB\": {\"compliantCount\": X, \"nonCompliantCount\": Y, \"noQualificationsCount\": Z}, \"GroupC\": {\"compliantCount\": X, \"nonCompliantCount\": Y, \"noQualificationsCount\": Z} }\nData Input: {{$context.$nodes.DATA_CLASSIFICATION_AGENT.$output}}\n\nPlease generate a bar chart based on this data with the following specifications:\n\tStructure:\n\t\tSort the groups in ascending order based on their names.\n\t\tName each bar as below it's group name from the input.\n\t\tName noQualificationsCount as \"New suppliers\".\n\t\tName sum of nonCompliantCount and noQualificationsCount as \"Non-compliant\".\n\t\tName sum of compliantCount, nonCompliantCount and noQualificationsCount as \"Total\".\n\t\tFor each Group on the X-axis, display two separate bars side-by-side:\n\t\t\t1. One bar always representing 100 as the \"Total\" count in percentage(%). \n\t\t\t2. Second bar representing the percentage of \"Non-compliant\" count in percentage(%) calculating corresponding to the value of \"Total\" for that bar as 100 percentage(%).\n\n\tColors:\n\t\tUse Blue for the \"Total\" bars and Red for the \"Non-compliant\" bars.\n\n\tY-Axis:\n\t\t- y-axis min = 0\n\t\t- y-axis max = 100\n\t\t- y-axis interval/tick step = 10\n\t\tDo not rely on automatic scaling. Always hardcode these values.\n\n\tLabels:\n\t\tInclude a legend to identify bars for \"Non-compliant\" and \"Total\".\n\t\tPlace the specific percentage value at each data point on top of each bar, calculating \"Total\" as 100% and \"Non-compliant\" as the corresponding percentage of \"Total\" for each bar. \n\n\tTitle:\n\t\tSet the title of the chart as \"Supplier Compliance Coverage\" strictly. Do not add any additional thing to the title. \n\n\tSub Title: \n\t\tShow \"Supplier qualification performance across tiers, showing coverage and potential gaps.\" as the subtitle. Do not add any additional thing to it.\n\n\tKey Insights:\n\t\tadd three parts, \"Overall Qualification Health\", \"Risk concentration\" and \"Recommended Action\", as key insights.\n\t\tFollow below rules to identify key insights.\n\t\tFor Overall Qualification Health:\n\t\t    Show overall qualification levels as Strong, Moderate or Weak based on the numbers of qualified vs total suppliers in each tiers. Also justify your statement using actual numbers.\n\t\t    Example: Qualification compliance is highest among Tier A suppliers and declines across lower tiers. Non-compliance impacts 20 of 100 Tier A suppliers, 35 of 100 Tier B suppliers, and 45 of 100 Tier C suppliers.\n\t\tFor Risk concentration:\n\t\t    Identify the supplier group contributing the highest number or percentage of Non-compliant suppliers. Do not mention the count or percentage.\n\t\t    Example: Tier C has the lowest qualification rate, indicating localized compliance risk.\n\t\tFor Recommended Action:\n\t\t    Determine remediation action based on \"New suppliers\"(noQualificationsCount) and \"Not qualified suppliers\"(nonCompliantCount) count within each group. Do not use noQualificationsCount and nonCompliantCount words or counts.\n\t\t    Examples: Prioritize remediation for Tier C while maintaining controls across other tiers.\n        \nDo not ask clarifying questions. Present the chart based on the data provided."
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n  \"title\": \"Supplier Compliance Coverage\",\n  \"subtitle\": \"Supplier qualification performance across tiers, showing coverage and potential gaps.\",\n  \"data\": {\n    \"labels\": [\"GroupA\", \"GroupB\", \"GroupC\"],\n    \"datasets\": [\n      {\n        \"label\": \"Total\",\n        \"data\": [100, 100, 100],\n        \"backgroundColor\": \"blue\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"label\": \"Non-compliant\",\n        \"data\": [20, 30, 10],\n        \"backgroundColor\": \"red\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"options\": {\n    \"scales\": {\n      \"y\": {\n        \"min\": 0,\n        \"max\": 100,\n        \"ticks\": {\n          \"stepSize\": 10\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    \"plugins\": {\n      \"legend\": {\n        \"display\": true\n      },\n      \"datalabels\": {\n        \"anchor\": \"end\",\n        \"align\": \"top\",\n        \"formatter\": \"(value) => value + '%'\"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"insights\": {\n    \"Overall Qualification Health\": \"Qualification levels are strong overall, with most groups above 80% compliance.\",\n    \"Risk concentration\": \"GroupB has the highest proportion of non-compliant suppliers (30 of 100), indicating higher localized risk, followed by GroupA (20 of 100). GroupC shows minimal risk (10 of 100).\",\n    \"Priority focus\": \"Prioritize remediation efforts for GroupB due to higher unqualified and newly added suppliers, while maintaining steady controls in GroupA and GroupC.\"\n  }\n}",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
